1. Terraform configuration files
File	Purpose
resource.tf	Creates the Azure Resource Group
vnet.tf	Creates the Virtual Network
subnet.tf	Creates the Subnet
generic-variables.tf	Defines input variables
private-variables.tf	Defines local values
2. What is a Terraform Variable?

A variable is an input to your Terraform configuration.

For example:

variable "location" {
  description = "location where resource will be created"
  type        = string
  default     = "eastus"
}

You use it inside a resource with:

location = var.location

So:

variable "location"
       ↓
   var.location
       ↓
     eastus
       ↓
Azure Resource
Why use variables?

Instead of hardcoding:

location = "eastus"

you can write:

location = var.location

Now the same Terraform code can be used for:

Development → eastus
Testing     → westus
Production  → centralus

without modifying the resource code.

3. What is a Local Block?

A locals block creates named expressions that you can reuse inside your Terraform configuration.

Your example:

locals {
  name_prefix = "${var.environment}-${var.owner}"


  project_tags = {
    Environment = "dev"
    Owner       = "002"
    project     = "dispatch"
  }
}

The important point is:

locals
   │
   ├── name_prefix
   │
   └── project_tags

You access them using:

local.name_prefix

and:

local.project_tags
Example

If:

environment = "dev"
owner       = "gopal"

then:

name_prefix = "${var.environment}-${var.owner}"

produces:

dev-gopal

Then this:

name = "${local.name_prefix}-${var.resource_group_name}"

could produce:

dev-gopal-gopal-rg
4. Variable vs Local
Variable	Local
Input to Terraform	Named expression inside Terraform
Can be supplied by user	Calculated/defined inside configuration
var.location	local.name_prefix
Useful for customization	Useful for reuse
Example: eastus	Example: dev-gopal

Think of it simply as:

VARIABLE
   ↓
Input


LOCAL
   ↓
Process / combine / reuse

For example:

variable "environment" {
  default = "dev"
}


variable "owner" {
  default = "gopal"
}


locals {
  name_prefix = "${var.environment}-${var.owner}"
}

Result:

environment ──┐
              ├──> local.name_prefix ──> dev-gopal
owner ────────┘
5. Resource Reference Block

Your resource is:

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "myrg" {
  name     = "${local.name_prefix}-${var.resource_group_name}"
  location = var.location
  tags     = local.project_tags
}

Here:

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "myrg"

has two important names:

azurerm_resource_group
        ↓
Terraform resource type


myrg
 ↓
User-defined reference name

Therefore:

azurerm_resource_group.myrg.name

means:

Get the name attribute of the Resource Group represented by the myrg reference.

6. Resource-to-Resource Dependency

Your VNet contains:

location = azurerm_resource_group.myrg.location


resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.myrg.name

Terraform understands that the VNet depends on the Resource Group.

The relationship becomes:

Resource Group
      │
      ▼
Virtual Network
      │
      ▼
Subnet

This is one of the major advantages of Terraform: you reference resources rather than manually determining their creation order.

7. Complete Execution Workflow

Use this sequence in your lab:

1. Edit Terraform files
        ↓
2. Define variables
        ↓
3. Define locals
        ↓
4. terraform init
        ↓
5. terraform validate
        ↓
6. terraform plan
        ↓
7. Review the plan
        ↓
8. terraform apply
        ↓
9. Azure creates:
       ├── Resource Group
       ├── Virtual Network
       └── Subnet
        ↓
10. Verify in Azure Portal

If you are intentionally changing an existing environment, you can first run:

terraform destroy

and then:

terraform apply

For normal Terraform changes, however, you generally do not need to destroy everything first. terraform plan will show what Terraform intends to change.

Key concept
var.*     → INPUT VALUES


local.*   → REUSABLE/CALCULATED VALUES


resource.*.* → REFERENCES TO CREATED RESOURCES

So your configuration effectively works like:

generic-variables.tf
        │
        ▼
    var.location
    var.owner
    var.environment
        │
        ▼
private-variables.tf
        │
        ▼
local.name_prefix
local.project_tags
        │
        ▼
resource.tf / vnet.tf / subnet.tf
        │
        ▼
      Terraform
        │
        ▼
       Azure

This is the core concept behind making Terraform code dynamic, reusable, consistent, and easier to maintain.
